;; ============================================================================

;; THE SCOPE NODE  —  port of Simple's `node/ScopeNode.java` and `Var.java`
;;
;; SSA construction, done DURING parsing. There is no separate SSA pass.
;; ============================================================================
;;
;; ## Why a scope is a NODE
;;
;; It is not part of the final graph — the parser discards it. It is a node so that a variable
;; binding is an ORDINARY USE-DEF EDGE, which makes "read the current value of x" a single array
;; index instead of a symbol-table walk, and makes the scope a real user of every value it holds so
;; nothing it binds can be collected out from under it.
;;
;; Slot 0 is control. Slot 1 is memory. Slots 2.. are the variables, and `_vars` maps 1-to-1 onto
;; the inputs array. Memory is deliberately just another parser-state value in a slot: at a loop
;; head it gets the SAME lazy-Phi treatment as any variable, with no special case.
;;
;; ## LAZY PHIS — the trick that makes this affordable
;;
;; `dup` does NOT create a Phi per variable. It copies the bindings, and for a loop it stores a
;; SENTINEL — the scope itself — in each non-final slot. Reading through a sentinel triggers Phi
;; creation ON DEMAND, so a Phi appears only for a variable actually assigned on more than one path.
;;
;; Without this, a loop over a scope with fifty live variables creates fifty Phis on entry and the
;; peepholes spend their time deleting forty-nine of them. Final variables skip the sentinel
;; entirely — they cannot be reassigned, so they can never need a Phi.
;;
;; `dup` also `keep()`s every non-CFG guard, so that the accounting still balances when the guards
;; are later removed together.
;;
;; ## THE LOOP PROTOCOL — get this wrong and the loop vanishes silently
;;
;;     1. `scope-dup` the entry scope for the body, with the loop sentinel
;;     2. build the body, INSIDE the Loop's in-progress window
;;     3. peephole the body — still inside the window
;;     4. `scope-end-loop!` — as one protected operation, wire the Loop control back edge and then
;;        fill every materialized Phi's back edge before exposing the finished scope
;;
;; Final Simple's `endLoop` asserts `loop.inProgress()` on entry, sets the control back edge first,
;; fills the Phi back edges immediately afterward, kills the back-edge scope, and then does a
;; one-time useless-Phi removal. Nothing may run a peephole between those two edge-filling steps.
;;
;; `_endLoop` has one subtlety worth copying exactly: if a variable's DECLARED type is
;; high-or-constant and its current binding is not an in-progress Phi, no Phi is made at all —
;; the value is subsumed by the constant, because nothing can lift higher than a constant.
;;
;; The live Phi implementation independently treats a null final value as in progress, so even an
;; accidentally exposed intermediate state cannot fold. That is a safety net around Simple's
;; atomic parser protocol, not permission for callers to split `scope-end-loop!` into two phases.
;;
;; ## MERGING AT A JOIN
;;
;; `mergeScopes` builds a Region over the two control edges, then `_merge` walks the slots and
;; creates a Phi ONLY where the two scopes disagree. A name can only differ at the innermost level
;; where it is bound, which is what makes the walk linear rather than a per-level search.
;;
;; ## GUARDS — where a TypeScript annotation and a runtime `typeof` become the same mechanism
;;
;; `addGuards` refines the bindings on the edge where a predicate is known true. After
;; `if (typeof x === "number")`, the slot for `x` ON THAT EDGE holds a Cast, so every later read
;; gets the narrowed value with no further analysis. Simple's example is `if (ptr) ptr.field;` being
;; legal because `ptr` is known non-null there.
;;
;; Simple is candid about the limits, and we inherit them rather than pretending otherwise:
;;
;; > "This operation is LEXICAL, not semantic, and thus its behavior changes the language spec.
;; > Optimizations that hide the lexical structure (e.g. constant folding control flow) can hide
;; > what is guarded or how the guard is applied, making it somewhat fragile."
;;
;; Its known gap: `if (!ary || !ary#) exit();` guards the whole expression but loses that `ary`
;; alone is non-null. Simple suggests a dedicated logical-expression node or a gather-of-checked-
;; values. For TypeScript this matters more than it does for Simple, because narrowing is a language
;; feature users rely on — so this is a place we will likely need to go beyond Simple, and that
;; will be a decision to record rather than a drift.
;;
;; ## Var
;;
;; Simple's comment: "The tracked fields are now complex enough to deserve a array-of-structs
;; layout." Declared name and type, slot index, final, forward-reference, and a parser-only
;; definite-initialisation marker. The `uninit` flag is parser-only because the binding's NULLABLE
;; TYPE carries the path-sensitive state through SSA merges — the type system does the work, and
;; the flag only catches the syntactic case.
;;
;; STATUS: lexical levels, ordinary bindings, branch duplication/merge, bulk memory, lazy loop
;; Phis, loop closure, and direct truthiness guards are implemented for the admitted frontend.
;; Four final-Simple facilities remain outside this boundary and are not silently approximated:
;; forward-reference creation is handled instead by the frontend's declaration-header pass,
;; `_endLoop`'s immediate one-shot useless-Phi cleanup is left to the normal Iter fixpoint, and
;; its declared high/constant loop-slot substitution is unnecessary while frontend bindings are
;; declared dynamic; guard discovery also does not yet recurse through Simple's short-circuit Phi
;; patterns. `fref` remains reserved for that forward-reference protocol.
;;
;; JavaScript divergence: lexical declarations are instantiated before statement evaluation.
;; Simple's define checks current-level duplicates, appends Var and its value edge, and update
;; resolves loop sentinels before replacing a mutable binding. We retain that order, but split
;; lexical creation from initialization. An uninitialized slot holds lattice TOP, not JavaScript
;; undefined; lookup/update must refuse it before lazy-Phi resolution. Duplicate scopes copy the
;; marker and never give such slots loop sentinels. In the admitted structured grammar a binding
;; initializes only in its own statement list, so surviving bindings agree at joins. Captured TDZ
;; and executable ReferenceError edges are UNIMPLEMENTED, not nullable-type approximations.
(module aot.node.scope)
(import "coil.primitive" :as primitive)
(import "coil.alloc" :as alloc :use [unwrap-ptr create box!])
(import "coil.arraylist" :use *)
(import "aot.util.panic" :use *)
(import "aot.node.node" :use *)
(import "aot.node.control" :use *)
(import "aot.node.constant" :use *)
(import "aot.node.phi" :use *)
(import "aot.node.memory" :use *)
(import "aot.node.dynamic" :use *)
(import "aot.type.type" :use *)

;; Fixed slots. Everything from SCOPE-VAR0 up is a variable.
(const SCOPE-CTRL 0)

(const SCOPE-MEM 1)

(const SCOPE-VAR0 2)

;; A tracked binding. Array-of-structs because the parser touches all of these together.
(defstruct Var
  [(name (slice u8))
   (idx i64)  ; slot in the containing scope; matches the inputs array 1-to-1
   (declared i64)  ; declared type
   (final bool)  ; cannot be reassigned, so can never need a Phi
   (fref bool)  ; a forward reference, not yet resolved
   (uninit bool)])  ; lexical binding exists but InitializeBinding has not executed

;; Final Simple stores parser metadata on ScopeNode itself. Keeping it here—not in a second name
;; map owned by lowering—is what makes the Scope the one authoritative SSA environment.
(defstruct ScopeNode
  [(hdr NodeHdr)
   (vars (ArrayList Var))
   (levels (ArrayList i64))
   (guards (ArrayList (dyn NodeOps)))])

(defn scope-node [(s (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (ptr ScopeNode))
  (if (!= (n-op s) OP-SCOPE)
      (do
        (panic "scope-node: node is not a Scope")
        (primitive/cast (ptr ScopeNode) (hdr (unreachable-node))))
      (primitive/cast (ptr ScopeNode) (hdr s))))

(impl NodeOps ScopeNode
  (hdr [(self (ptr ScopeNode))] (-> (ptr NodeHdr)) (primitive/cast (ptr NodeHdr) self))
  (label [(self (ptr ScopeNode))] (-> (slice u8)) "Scope")
  ;; Scope is parser state and is never optimized as program data.
  (compute [(self (ptr ScopeNode))] (-> i64) TY-TOP)
  (idealize [(self (ptr ScopeNode))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps))) (None [(dyn NodeOps)]))
  ;; Distinct live environments must never GVN, even if their current bindings happen to match.
  (eq-extra [(self (ptr ScopeNode)) (other (dyn NodeOps))] (-> bool) false)
  (hash-extra [(self (ptr ScopeNode))] (-> i64) (.nid (.hdr self)))
  (is-const? [(self (ptr ScopeNode))] (-> bool) false)
  (no-const-fold? [(self (ptr ScopeNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (is-pinned? [(self (ptr ScopeNode))] (-> bool) true)
  (binop-copy [(self (ptr ScopeNode)) (lhs (dyn NodeOps)) (rhs (dyn NodeOps))]
              (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
              (None [(dyn NodeOps)])))

(defn scope-var-count [(s (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (len (.vars (scope-node s))))

(defn scope-var [(s (dyn NodeOps)) (i i64)] (-> Var)
  (get (.vars (scope-node s)) i))

(defn scope-var-new [(name (slice u8))
                     (idx i64)
                     (declared i64)
                     (final bool)
                     (fref bool)
                     (uninit bool)] (-> Var)
  (let [(mut v) (primitive/zeroed Var)]
    (set! (.name (mut v)) name)
    (set! (.idx (mut v)) idx)
    (set! (.declared (mut v)) declared)
    (set! (.final (mut v)) final)
    (set! (.fref (mut v)) fref)
    (set! (.uninit (mut v)) uninit)
    (load v)))

(defn scope-find [(s (dyn NodeOps)) (name (slice u8))] (-> i64)
  (let [(mut i) (- (scope-var-count s) 1)]
    (loop (if (< (load i) 0)
              (break -1)
              (let [v (scope-var s (load i))]
                (if (= (.name v) name)
                    (break (load i))
                    (do (set! (mut i) (- (load i) 1)) (continue))))))))

(defn scope-make [] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [a (alloc/malloc-allocator)
        p (alloc/unwrap-ptr [ScopeNode] (alloc/create [ScopeNode] a))
        d (primitive/make-dyn NodeOps p)]
    (set! (.vars p) (al-new [Var] a))
    (set! (.levels p) (al-new [i64] a))
    (set! (.guards p) (al-new [(dyn NodeOps)] a))
    (n-init! d OP-SCOPE)
    d))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Lexical nesting
(defn scope-push! [(s (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (push! [i64] (mut (.levels (scope-node s))) (n-nins s)))

(defn scope-pop! [(s (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (match (pop! (mut (.levels (scope-node s))))
    (None [] (broken "scope-pop!: no lexical scope"))
    (Some [base]
          (do
            (n-pop-until! s base)
            (loop (if (<= (scope-var-count s) (- base SCOPE-VAR0))
                      (break 0)
                      (do (pop! (mut (.vars (scope-node s)))) (continue))))
            0))))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Bindings
(defn scope-define! [(s (dyn NodeOps))
                     (name (slice u8))
                     (declared i64)
                     (final bool)
                     (init (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (let [p (scope-node s)
        nlevels (len (.levels p))
        base
          (if (= nlevels 0)
              SCOPE-VAR0
              (get (.levels p) (- nlevels 1)))
        (mut i) (- (n-nins s) 1)]
    (loop (if (< (load i) base)
              (break 0)
              (let [v (scope-var s (- (load i) SCOPE-VAR0))]
                (if (= (.name v) name)
                    (broken "scope-define!: duplicate binding in lexical scope")
                    (do (set! (mut i) (- (load i) 1)) (continue))))))
    (let [idx (n-nins s)]
      (push! [Var] (mut (.vars p)) (scope-var-new name idx declared final false false))
      (n-add-def! s (Some init))
      idx)))

(defn scope-declare! [(s (dyn NodeOps)) (name (slice u8)) (declared i64) (final bool)] (-> i64)
  (let [idx (scope-define! s name declared final (con-make TY-TOP))
        meta (al-elem [Var] (mut (.vars (scope-node s))) (- idx SCOPE-VAR0))]
    (set! (.uninit meta) true)
    idx))

(defn scope-initialize! [(s (dyn NodeOps)) (name (slice u8)) (value (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (let [i (scope-find s name)]
    (if (< i 0)
        (broken "scope-initialize!: binding was not instantiated")
        (let [meta (al-elem [Var] (mut (.vars (scope-node s))) i)]
          (if (not (.uninit meta))
              (broken "scope-initialize!: binding already initialized")
              (do
                (set! (.uninit meta) false)
                (n-set-def! s (+ SCOPE-VAR0 i) (Some value))))))))

(defn scope-require-initialized! [(s (dyn NodeOps)) (i i64)] (-> i64)
  (let [meta (scope-var s i)]
    (if (.uninit meta)
        (unimplemented "lexical temporal-dead-zone ReferenceError"
                       "docs/GAPS.md — executable exception completions")
        0)))

;; Resolve one lazy loop sentinel. The first read or write creates the Phi in the loop-head Scope;
;; nested loop sentinels recurse outward exactly as final Simple's `update(var,null)` does.
(defn scope-resolve-slot! [(s (dyn NodeOps)) (idx i64)] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [old (n-in! s idx)]
    (if (!= (n-op old) OP-SCOPE)
        old
        (let [head old
              v
                (if (= idx SCOPE-MEM)
                    (scope-var-new "$mem" SCOPE-MEM (ty-mem ALIAS-BULK TY-BOT) false false false)
                    (scope-var s (- idx SCOPE-VAR0)))
              declared (.declared v)
              name (.name v)
              current (n-in! head idx)]
          (if (and (or (= (n-op current) OP-PHI) (= (n-op current) OP-MEMPHI))
                   (n-same? (phi-region current) (scope-ctrl head)))
              (do (n-set-def! s idx (Some current)) current)
              (let [incoming (scope-resolve-slot! head idx)
                    p
                      (if (= idx SCOPE-MEM)
                          (memphi-make (scope-ctrl head) (ty-mem-alias declared) (ty-mem-elem declared) name)
                          (phi-make (scope-ctrl head) declared name))]
                (if (= idx SCOPE-MEM) (memphi-add-state! p incoming) (phi-add-value! p incoming))
                (if (= idx SCOPE-MEM) (memphi-open! p) (phi-open! p))
                (n-set-def! head idx (Some p))
                (n-set-def! s idx (Some p))
                p))))))

;; Follow lazy sentinels without materializing a Phi. Loop finalization uses this for slots that
;; were never touched in the body: their exit value is exactly the pre-loop definition.
(defn scope-entry-value [(s (dyn NodeOps)) (idx i64)] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [n (n-in! s idx)]
    (if (= (n-op n) OP-SCOPE) (scope-entry-value n idx) n)))

(defn scope-final-value [(n (dyn NodeOps)) (idx i64)] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (if (= (n-op n) OP-SCOPE) (scope-entry-value n idx) n))

;; Read a binding. Reading through a loop SENTINEL is what creates the lazy Phi.
(defn scope-lookup [(s (dyn NodeOps)) (name (slice u8))] (-> (Option (dyn NodeOps)))
  (let [i (scope-find s name)]
    (if (< i 0)
        (None [(dyn NodeOps)])
        (do
          (scope-require-initialized! s i)
          (Some (scope-resolve-slot! s (+ SCOPE-VAR0 i)))))))

(defn scope-update! [(s (dyn NodeOps)) (name (slice u8)) (v (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (let [i (scope-find s name)]
    (if (< i 0)
        (broken "scope-update!: unknown binding")
        (let [meta (scope-var s i)]
          (scope-require-initialized! s i)
          (if (.final meta)
              (broken "scope-update!: assignment to final binding")
              (do
                (scope-resolve-slot! s (+ SCOPE-VAR0 i))
                (n-set-def! s (+ SCOPE-VAR0 i) (Some v))))))))

(defn scope-ctrl [(s (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (n-in! s SCOPE-CTRL))

(defn scope-set-ctrl! [(s (dyn NodeOps)) (c (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (n-set-def! s SCOPE-CTRL (Some c)))

;; Memory is an ordinary slot, so it gets the lazy-Phi machinery for free.
(defn scope-mem [(s (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (scope-resolve-slot! s SCOPE-MEM))

(defn scope-set-mem! [(s (dyn NodeOps)) (m (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (do (scope-resolve-slot! s SCOPE-MEM) (n-set-def! s SCOPE-MEM (Some m))))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Duplication and merging

;; Duplicate for a branch or a loop body. When `for-loop`, non-final slots get the SENTINEL that
;; triggers lazy Phi creation on first read.
(defn scope-dup [(s (dyn NodeOps)) (for-loop bool)] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (let [d (scope-make)
        src (scope-node s)
        dst (scope-node d)]
    (al-for [v (.vars src)] (push! [Var] (mut (.vars dst)) v))
    (al-for [level (.levels src)] (push! [i64] (mut (.levels dst)) level))
    (al-for [guard (.guards src)]
            (do
              (if (= (n-op guard) OP-CPROJ) 0 (n-keep! guard))
              (push! [(dyn NodeOps)] (mut (.guards dst)) guard)))
    (n-add-def! d (Some (scope-ctrl s)))
    (let [(mut i) SCOPE-MEM]
      (loop (if (>= (load i) (n-nins s))
                (break 0)
                (let [meta
                        (if (= (load i) SCOPE-MEM)
                            (scope-var-new "$mem" SCOPE-MEM (ty-mem ALIAS-BULK TY-BOT) false false false)
                            (scope-var s (- (load i) SCOPE-VAR0)))
                      lazy (and for-loop (not (.final meta)) (not (.uninit meta)))]
                  (n-add-def! d (Some (if lazy s (n-in! s (load i)))))
                  (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                  (continue)))))
    d))

;; Build the Region and create a Phi only where the two scopes disagree.
(defn scope-merge! [(a (dyn NodeOps)) (b (dyn NodeOps))] (-> (dyn NodeOps))
  (if (!= (n-nins a) (n-nins b))
      (do (panic "scope-merge!: declarations are unbalanced") a)
      (let [r (region-make)]
        (region-add-path! r (scope-ctrl a))
        (region-add-path! r (scope-ctrl b))
        (scope-set-ctrl! a r)
        (let [(mut i) SCOPE-MEM]
          (loop (if (>= (load i) (n-nins a))
                    (break 0)
                    (let [lhs (scope-resolve-slot! a (load i))
                          rhs (scope-resolve-slot! b (load i))]
                      (if (>= (load i) SCOPE-VAR0)
                          (let [av (scope-var a (- (load i) SCOPE-VAR0))
                                bv (scope-var b (- (load i) SCOPE-VAR0))]
                            (if (!= (.uninit av) (.uninit bv))
                                (unimplemented "path-dependent lexical initialization at Scope merge"
                                               "docs/GAPS.md — executable TDZ state")
                                0))
                          0)
                      (if (n-same? lhs rhs)
                          0
                          (let [meta
                                  (if (= (load i) SCOPE-MEM)
                                      (scope-var-new "$mem" SCOPE-MEM (ty-mem ALIAS-BULK TY-BOT) false false false)
                                      (scope-var a (- (load i) SCOPE-VAR0)))
                                declared (.declared meta)
                                name (.name meta)
                                p
                                  (if (= (load i) SCOPE-MEM)
                                      (memphi-make r (ty-mem-alias declared) (ty-mem-elem declared) name)
                                      (phi-make r declared name))]
                            (if (= (load i) SCOPE-MEM)
                                (do (memphi-add-state! p lhs) (memphi-add-state! p rhs))
                                (do (phi-add-value! p lhs) (phi-add-value! p rhs)))
                            (n-set-def! a (load i) (Some p))))
                      (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                      (continue)))))
        (n-kill! b)
        a)))

;; Finalize Loop control and every materialized Phi back edge as one operation, kill the back-edge
;; scope, then remove useless Phis. No caller may observe or optimize the intermediate state.
(defn scope-end-loop! [(entry (dyn NodeOps)) (back (dyn NodeOps)) (exit (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (let [head (scope-ctrl entry)]
    (if (!= (n-op head) OP-LOOP)
        (broken "scope-end-loop!: entry control is not a Loop")
        (do
          ;; Java objects remain alive throughout Simple's `_endLoop`. Coil nodes instead use
          ;; eager use-count reclamation: resolving the back scope's memory sentinel can remove
          ;; the entry scope's last ordinary use while later variable slots still need it. Keep
          ;; the entry scope for the complete atomic operation, mirroring Simple's object lifetime.
          (n-keep! entry)
          ;; Final Simple's order is control first, then every open Phi without an intervening
          ;; peephole. Phi's null final input keeps this short window unobservable to optimization.
          (loop-close! head (scope-ctrl back))
          (let [(mut i) SCOPE-MEM]
            (loop (if (>= (load i) (n-nins entry))
                      (break 0)
                      (let [raw (n-in! entry (load i))
                            n (scope-final-value raw (load i))]
                        (if (not (n-same? raw n))
                            (n-set-def! entry (load i) (Some n))
                            0)
                        ;; Recursive sentinel resolution can expose an in-progress Phi owned by an
                        ;; enclosing loop. Final Simple closes a Phi only when its Region is this
                        ;; loop head; the enclosing loop will supply that Phi's backedge later.
                        (if (and (or (= (n-op n) OP-PHI) (= (n-op n) OP-MEMPHI))
                                 (phi-in-progress? n)
                                 (n-same? (phi-region n) head))
                            (let [back-value (scope-resolve-slot! back (load i))]
                              (do
                                (if (= (n-op n) OP-MEMPHI)
                                    (memphi-close! n back-value)
                                    (phi-close! n back-value))
                                0))
                            0)
                        (if (n-same? (n-in! exit (load i)) entry)
                            (n-set-def! exit (load i) (Some n))
                            0)
                      (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                      (continue)))))
          (let [(mut i) SCOPE-MEM]
            (loop (if (>= (load i) (n-nins entry))
                      (break 0)
                      (let [value (n-in! entry (load i))]
                        (if (and (or (= (n-op value) OP-PHI) (= (n-op value) OP-MEMPHI))
                                 (phi-in-progress? value)
                                 (n-same? (phi-region value) head))
                            (panic-i "scope-end-loop!: open Phi survived finalization at slot"
                                     (load i))
                            0)
                        (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                        (continue)))))
          (n-kill! back)
          (n-unkeep! entry)
          0))))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Guards — lexical narrowing on a tested edge
(defn scope-replace-binding! [(s (dyn NodeOps)) (old (dyn NodeOps)) (fresh (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (let [(mut i) SCOPE-VAR0]
    (loop (if (>= (load i) (n-nins s))
              (break 0)
              (do
                (if (n-same? (n-in! s (load i)) old)
                    (n-set-def! s (load i) (Some fresh))
                    0)
                (set! (mut i) (+ (load i) 1))
                (continue))))))

(defn scope-add-guards! [(s (dyn NodeOps))
                         (ctrl (dyn NodeOps))
                         (pred (dyn NodeOps))
                         (invert bool)] (-> i64)
  (if (!= (n-op ctrl) OP-CPROJ)
      (broken "scope-add-guards!: control is not an If projection")
      (let [truthy
              (if invert
                  (!= (cproj-idx ctrl) IF-TRUE-IDX)
                  (= (cproj-idx ctrl) IF-TRUE-IDX))
            narrowed (cast-make ctrl pred (truthiness-refine-ty (n-ty pred) truthy))]
        (push! [(dyn NodeOps)] (mut (.guards (scope-node s))) ctrl)
        (if (n-same? narrowed pred)
            0
            (do
              (n-keep! pred)
              (n-keep! narrowed)
              (push! [(dyn NodeOps)] (mut (.guards (scope-node s))) pred)
              (push! [(dyn NodeOps)] (mut (.guards (scope-node s))) narrowed)
              (scope-replace-binding! s pred narrowed)
              0)))))

(defn scope-remove-guards! [(s (dyn NodeOps)) (ctrl (dyn NodeOps))] (-> i64)
  (let [(mut done) false]
    (loop (if (load done)
              (break 0)
              (match (pop! (mut (.guards (scope-node s))))
                (None [] (broken "scope-remove-guards!: marker not found"))
                (Some [top]
                      (if (n-same? top ctrl)
                          (set! (mut done) true)
                          (match (pop! (mut (.guards (scope-node s))))
                            (None [] (broken "scope-remove-guards!: guard pair is incomplete"))
                            (Some [pred]
                                  (do
                                    (scope-replace-binding! s top pred)
                                    (n-unkeep! top)
                                    (n-unkeep! pred)
                                    0))))))))
    0))
